[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the SwiftUI Layout System: Proposals, Sizes, and Coordinate Spaces"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SwiftUI abandons the imperative frame-based layout of UIKit in favor of a declarative, data-driven layout engine. To build robust custom layouts and master existing ones, you must understand the three core pillars of the layout cycle: Proposals, Sizes, and Coordinate Spaces."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"The Three-Phase Layout Cycle"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike UIKit's multi-pass layout system involving layoutSubviews and constraints, SwiftUI executes a simple, elegant three-step process for every frame rendering:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. Proposal: The parent proposes a size (width and height) to its child view."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. Sizing: The child chooses its own size based on that proposal and its internal content."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. Positioning: The parent places the child at a specific coordinate point within its own space."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Golden Rule of SwiftUI Layout"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The fundamental mantra of SwiftUI layout is: "Parent proposes size, child chooses size, parent decides position." A child view ultimately has the final say on its dimensions, provided they fit within the constraints offered by the parent."},{"order":10,"type":"heading","text":"Size Proposals in Action"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"A size proposal is represented by the 'ProposedViewSize' struct. It can contain specific CGFloat values for width and height, or it can be 'nil', indicating an unspecified dimension, or '.infinity', indicating that the parent encourages the child to take up as much space as possible."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"struct ProposalInspector: View {
    var body: some View {
        Color.blue
            .frame(width: 200, height: 100)
            .fixedSize()
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Layouts using the Layout Protocol"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in iOS 16, the 'Layout' protocol allows developers to write custom layout containers by implementing two essential methods: 'sizeThatFits(proposal:subviews:cache:)' and 'placeSubviews(in:proposal:subviews:cache:)'."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"struct FlowLayout: Layout {
    func sizeThatFits(proposal: ProposedViewSize, subviews: Subviews, cache: inout ()) -> CGSize {
        // Calculate and return the total size needed for all subviews
        return proposal.replacingUnspecifiedDimensions()
    }

    func placeSubviews(in bounds: CGRect, proposal: ProposedViewSize, subviews: Subviews, cache: inout ()) {
        for subview in subviews {
            // Position each subview explicitly
            subview.place(at: bounds.origin, proposal: ProposedViewSize(bounds.size))
        }
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Working with Coordinate Spaces"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Coordinate spaces in SwiftUI are crucial when you need to track a view's position relative to another view, the screen, or a scroll offset. Historically handled via geometry readers, SwiftUI now provides modern APIs like 'geometryEffect' and coordinate space identifiers."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"struct CoordinateSpaceExample: View {
    var body: some View {
        ScrollView {
            VStack {
                Text("Tracking Scroll Position")
                    .background(
                        GeometryReader { proxy in
                            Color.clear
                                .preference(key: ScrollOffsetKey.self, value: proxy.frame(in: .named("ScrollSpace")).minY)
                        }
                    )
            }
        }
        .coordinateSpace(name: "ScrollSpace")
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering SwiftUI layout requires shifting your mindset from setting absolute frames to negotiating sizes via proposals. By understanding how parents propose sizes, how children respond, and how coordinate spaces map these elements, you can build flexible, adaptive, and highly performant user interfaces."}]
